# Laravel Model Saving Data Without Primary Key

When a model lacks an auto-incrementing primary key, standard save operations can fail silently or throw cryptic SQL errors if you aren't aware of how Eloquent handles identity columns. Consider a scenario where you have two models: `Profile` and `Student`. The `Profile` table has a standard `id` column. The `Student` table, however, does not define its own primary key; instead, it relies entirely on `profile_id` as a foreign key linking back to the `Profile.id`.

In this setup, you might attempt to update a student's status by fetching an instance based on the associated profile ID and calling `save()`. If you encounter the error `Column not found: 1054 Unknown column 'id' in 'where clause'`, it is not because Eloquent cannot handle foreign keys. The error stems from how Eloquent constructs the `UPDATE` statement when it believes a primary key exists.

By default, Eloquent assumes every model has an `id` primary key that increments automatically unless explicitly told otherwise. When you call `$student->save()`, Eloquent generates an SQL query similar to this:

```sql
UPDATE students SET status = 1 WHERE id = [some_value]
```

Eloquent attempts to find the record by its default primary key name, `id`. However, since your `Student` model explicitly defines no primary key, that column does not exist in the table. This causes the database driver to throw the "Unknown column" error immediately.

To resolve this, you must inform Eloquent which column actually serves as the identity for your model. In older versions of Laravel (pre-5.2), this was done by setting a static property or using the `getKeyName` method depending on the specific version constraints. In modern Laravel (5.2+), the preferred approach is to define the primary key explicitly using the `$primaryKey` property, though legacy code often used `$primary_key = null`.

Update your `Student` model as follows:

```php
class Student extends Model
{
    protected $primaryKey = 'profile_id';

    public $incrementing = false;

    // Ensure mass assignment protection is handled correctly if needed
    protected $fillable = ['username', 'password', 'status', 'profile_id'];

    public function profile()
    {
        return $this->belongsTo('Profile');
    }
}
```

By setting `$primaryKey = 'profile_id'`, you instruct Eloquent to use the `profile_id` column as the unique identifier for all queries involving the model's existence. Consequently, when you run `$student->save()`, Eloquent will generate:

```sql
UPDATE students SET status = 1 WHERE profile_id = [actual_profile_id_value]
```

This matches your database schema perfectly. Note that because `profile_id` is likely a foreign key referencing another table, it usually does not auto-increment. Therefore, setting `$incrementing = false` is critical here to prevent Laravel from trying to append an `AUTO_INCREMENT` keyword or expecting a numeric sequence that doesn't exist.

If you are fetching the student in your controller using `Student::where('profile_id', '=', $id)->first()`, ensure that the variable `$id` represents the actual foreign key value (the profile's ID) and not the primary key of the parent table if you were querying via a relationship scope incorrectly. In your specific case, since you are passing the profile ID directly into the `where` clause, the logic holds up once the model knows to use that column for its own identity.

Another common pitfall involves mass assignment. If `$profile_id` is included in the fillable array but you are updating an instance fetched from the database, Eloquent will only update attributes listed in `$fillable`. Since `status` is also in the list, your update should work seamlessly once the primary key issue is resolved.

This pattern of using foreign keys as primary keys is less common than auto-incrementing IDs, but it is a valid design choice for many-to-one relationships where you only need one record per parent entity. Just remember that any relationship defined on other models must also respect this change. For instance, if another model has a `hasMany` relationship to students, the inverse side of that relationship must correctly reference the new primary key structure if it ever queries the students table directly.

For more details on configuring Eloquent relationships and identity columns in complex schemas, you may want to consult resources like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) which often cover advanced ORM configurations.
